          "text": "Excellent confirmation! The edge N/NW region shows:\n- **85\u00b0C + 0.75V: 74.8% yield** \u2190 THE EXCURSION\n- 25\u00b0C + 0.75V: 98.65% yield \u2713\n- 0\u00b0C + 0.75V: 99.72% yield \u2713\n- 85\u00b0C + 0.8V: 99.46% yield \u2713\n- 85\u00b0C + 0.85V: 99.73% yield \u2713\n\nThe issue is **exclusively** at the three-way combination. This indicates a localized physical defect that manifests under the combined stress of **high temperature AND low voltage**, specifically concentrated in the **northwest corner and adjacent areas**.\n\n---\n\n## ROOT_CAUSE_DIMENSIONS: TEMP_C, VDD, SPATIAL\n\n## ROOT_CAUSE_VALUES: TEMP_C=85; VDD=0.75; SPATIAL=edge N, NW\n\n## ROOT_CAUSE_STRUCTURE: interaction\n\n## ROOT_CAUSE_EVIDENCE: measured\n\n---\n\n## FINDINGS\n\n1. Overall lot yield is 91.6% with 315 failed parts (out of 3,750), measurement fail rate 0.361% (get_overall_yield baseline). Top failing tests are performance/voltage-marginal: core_fmax 0.764%, pll_lock 0.711%, vmin_core 0.684%, sram_bist 0.622% (get_failures_by_test).\n\n2. At 85\u00b0C only: yield drops to 94.08% with 222 failed parts and 0.805% fail rate (get_overall_yield TEMP_C=85). At 0.75V only: yield drops to 94.29% with 214 failed parts and 0.783% fail rate (get_overall_yield VDD=0.75). Each dimension alone degrades yield ~6%.\n\n3. Combined at 85\u00b0C + 0.75V: yield crashes to 94.91% with 187 failed parts and 2.126% fail rate (get_overall_yield TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75), a 5.1x increase in measurement fail rate vs. room-temperature baseline of 0.171%.\n\n4. Spatial stratification at 85\u00b0C + 0.75V shows edge region yield 92.11% vs. center 99.49% (get_overall_yield TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75, REGION). Within edge, NW octant yield is only 71.07% (57 failures, 12.41% fail rate) and N octant 79.07% (36 failures, 10.42% fail rate), while NE octant at same edge location is 100% yield (get_overall_yield TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75, REGION=edge, OCTANT variations).\n\n5. Combined NW + N edge at 85\u00b0C + 0.75V: 74.8% yield (93 failures, 11.505% fail rate) with core_fmax at 32.8% fail rate, vmin_core at 30.7%, pll_lock at 26.6%, and sram_bist at 23.1% (get_overall_yield and get_failures_by_test TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75, REGION=edge, OCTANT=[N,NW]).\n\n6. The excursion is **exclusive** to the 85\u00b0C + 0.75V combination: edge N/NW yield reaches 98.65% at 25\u00b0C + 0.75V, 99.72% at 0\u00b0C + 0.75V, 99.46% at 85\u00b0C + 0.8V, and 99.73% at 85\u00b0C + 0.85V (get_overall_yield temperature and voltage variations on edge N/NW region).\n\n## RULED OUT\n\n- Individual temperature effect: While 85\u00b0C alone reduces yield to 94.08%, the 0.8V and 0.85V supply voltages at 85\u00b0C both yield >99.4%, eliminating temperature as a sole cause.\n- Individual voltage effect: While 0.75V alone reduces yield to 94.29%, the same voltage at 0\u00b0C and 25\u00b0C yields >98.6%, eliminating voltage as a sole cause.\n- Wafer-wide process variation: The spatial concentration in NW and N edge positions, with NE edge at 100% under identical conditions, rules out global process skew.\n- Random defect: Random defects would not show this precise three-way correlation (temperature, voltage, spatial location).\n\n## ASSESSMENT\n\n**High confidence** that this is a localized physical defect (likely a process anomaly, layout defect, or thermal hotspot) in the **NW and N regions** of the wafer, concentrated at the **edge**. The defect is **dormant at room temperature or higher supply voltage** but becomes active under the combined stress of **hot temperature (85\u00b0C) and minimum supply voltage (0.75V)**, causing **performance and voltage margin test failures** (core_fmax, vmin_core, pll_lock, sram_bist).\n\nThe pattern is measured, not inferred: we quantified the 74.8% yield specifically at the intersection of all three dimensions (TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75, SPATIAL=edge N/NW). However, we can only correlate these dimensions, not establish the physical root cause from ATE data alone.\n\n## RECOMMENDED NEXT STEP\n\n1. **Physical inspection** of the NW and N edge regions of wafers from this lot using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) or focused ion beam (FIB) to identify process defects (voids, metal shorts, oxide defects, or lithography anomalies).\n\n2. **Thermal mapping** of the die and package under 85\u00b0C stress to confirm whether the NW corner experiences a localized thermal gradient that could exacerbate a marginal circuit.\n\n3. **Layout review** of the NW corner and adjacent metal/poly routing in the design to identify whether specific circuits (core PLL, timing paths) are concentrated there.\n\n4. **Trend analysis**: Determine whether this lot is isolated or part of an ongoing manufacturing condition. If ongoing, escalate to fab quality/process engineering immediately.\n\n5. **Scrap decision**: The 74.8% yield in the worst region (edge N/NW) may justify selective scrapping of dies from those locations, or full lot rejection depending on yield targets."
